Straightforward bootloader configuration. FIPS-encrypted Rocky on hd1, booting from USB GRUB. Let's build the correct GRUB stanza.
What GRUB Needs To Know

GRUB on USB sees your drives as:

    hd0 — USB drive (the bootloader source)
    hd1 — Rocky Linux encrypted drive (the target)

Step 1 — Find Your Partition Layout

At the GRUB prompt type:
grub

ls

This lists all detected devices. Then probe hd1:
grub

ls (hd1,gpt1)/
ls (hd1,gpt2)/

Typically Rocky with FIPS encryption lays out as:

    (hd1,gpt1) — /boot — unencrypted
    (hd1,gpt2) — LUKS encrypted root

Step 2 — Identify The Boot Partition
grub

ls (hd1,gpt1)/
# Should show: grub2/ vmlinuz-* initramfs-*

If you see those files, gpt1 is your /boot.
Step 3 — GRUB Boot Stanza

At the GRUB prompt:
grub

insmod part_gpt
insmod cryptodisk
insmod luks2
insmod gcry_rijndael
insmod gcry_sha256
insmod ext2

cryptomount (hd1,gpt2)
# GRUB will prompt for LUKS passphrase here

set root=(crypto0)

linux /vmlinuz-$(ls (hd1,gpt1)/vmlinuz* | tail -1) \
    root=/dev/mapper/rocky-root \
    ro \
    rd.luks.uuid=YOUR-LUKS-UUID \
    rd.lvm.lv=rocky/root \
    fips=1 \
    boot=UUID=YOUR-BOOT-UUID

initrd /initramfs-$(ls (hd1,gpt1)/initramfs* | tail -1).img

boot

Step 4 — Get Your UUIDs

From a live environment or another TTY:
bash

# LUKS UUID
cryptsetup luksDump /dev/sdb2 | grep UUID

# Boot partition UUID  
blkid /dev/sdb1

Step 5 — Simpler If /boot Is Separate

If GRUB can read (hd1,gpt1) directly:
grub

insmod part_gpt
insmod cryptodisk
insmod luks2
insmod gcry_rijndael
insmod gcry_sha256

cryptomount (hd1,gpt2)

set root=(hd1,gpt1)

configfile (hd1,gpt1)/grub2/grub.cfg

This hands off to Rocky's own grub.cfg which already has the correct kernel parameters including FIPS mode built in during installation.

That last configfile command is the cleanest path — it lets Rocky's own bootloader config handle everything including FIPS and LUKS parameters correctly.

