Jun 3

Straightforward GRUB device mapping issue. When you specify root=/dev/mapper/rl-root the kernel initializer is looking for that mapper device on whatever it considers the primary device — which defaults to hd0.
The Problem

The kernel rd.luks and rd.lvm parameters need to reference the LUKS UUID specifically, not the device path. Device paths are assigned at boot time and hd1 may enumerate differently to the kernel than to GRUB.
The Fix — Use UUIDs Not Device Paths

First get the LUKS UUID from GRUB prompt:
grub

ls -l (hd1,gpt2)

This returns the UUID of the LUKS container. Note it down.

Then build the linux line using UUID reference instead of device path:
grub

linux (hd1,gpt1)/vmlinuz-[version] \
    rd.luks.uuid=YOUR-LUKS-UUID \
    rd.lvm.lv=rl/root \
    root=/dev/mapper/rl-root \
    ro \
    fips=1 \
    boot=UUID=YOUR-BOOT-UUID

The rd.luks.uuid tells dracut exactly which device to unlock regardless of enumeration order. Once unlocked the mapper device appears correctly and root=/dev/mapper/rl-root resolves against it.
If UUID Is Not Visible At GRUB Prompt

From the GRUB command line:
grub

cryptomount (hd1,gpt2)
# Enter passphrase

ls -l (crypto0)

Note the UUID reported after successful cryptomount and use that in rd.luks.uuid.
Key Distinction
grub

# WRONG — device path, enumeration dependent
root=/dev/sdb2

# RIGHT — UUID, enumeration independent
rd.luks.uuid=xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx

