Standard FIPS boot path issue. The double slash and extra dot in the vmlinuz path is a grub.cfg path construction error.
The Problem

FIPS integrity check is building the path as:

/boot//.vmlinuz-6.x.x

Instead of:

/boot/vmlinuz-6.x.x

This means the boot variable has a trailing slash, and the vmlinuz entry has a leading dot — two separate issues combining.
Fix — Check grub.cfg Path Construction

In safe mode bash:
bash

cat /boot/grub2/grub.cfg | grep -i vmlinuz

Look for the linux line. It will show the malformed path.
Most Common Cause

The GRUB_PREFIX or root assignment has a trailing slash. Check:
bash

cat /boot/grub2/grubenv
cat /etc/default/grub | grep -i boot

Direct Fix

Edit grub.cfg and correct the path:
bash

# Find the broken line
grep -n "vmlinuz" /boot/grub2/grub.cfg

# Edit directly
vi /boot/grub2/grub.cfg

Find the line that reads something like:

linux /boot//.vmlinuz-6.x.x-xxx

Correct it to:

linux /vmlinuz-6.x.x-xxx

Note: when /boot is a separate partition and set as root in GRUB, the path should be /vmlinuz not /boot/vmlinuz — the /boot prefix is implicit from the root assignment.
Regenerate Correctly

