Standard LUKS double-prompt is a known issue when the UUID is declared without telling dracut which device to find it on. The // path error is GRUB not resolving the boot partition root correctly before handing to kernel.
Declare Boot UUID As Explicit Root
grub

insmod part_gpt
insmod cryptodisk
insmod luks2
insmod gcry_rijndael
insmod gcry_sha256
insmod xfs
insmod lvm

cryptomount (hd1,gpt2)

set root=(hd1,gpt1)
set prefix=(hd1,gpt1)/grub2

linux /vmlinuz-6.x.x-xxx \
    root=/dev/mapper/rl-root \
    rd.luks.uuid=YOUR-LUKS-UUID \
    rd.luks.options=tpm2-device=auto \
    rd.lvm.lv=rl/root \
    rd.lvm.vg=rl \
    ro \
    fips=1 \
    boot=UUID=YOUR-BOOT-UUID \
    BOOT_IMAGE=/vmlinuz-6.x.x-xxx

initrd /initramfs-6.x.x-xxx.img

boot

Key Additions

set prefix=(hd1,gpt1)/grub2 — tells GRUB exactly where its modules and config live, eliminates the // path construction error.

rd.lvm.vg=rl — explicitly declares the volume group name alongside the LV, stops dracut searching vg on wrong device.

BOOT_IMAGE= — explicitly sets the boot image path so FIPS integrity check knows exactly where to look. This is what fixes the // dot error in the FIPS check — it was constructing the path from an empty BOOT_IMAGE variable.
The Double Password Prompt

This happens because GRUB unlocks LUKS once via cryptomount and then dracut tries to unlock it again because rd.luks.uuid is declared without telling dracut GRUB already handled it.

Add this to the linux line:
grub

rd.luks.key=:existed

Or alternatively skip the second unlock entirely:
grub

rd.luks.options=allow-discards

