Standard FIPS noboot is a specific failure mode. It means dracut completed pre-boot but the FIPS integrity check on the kernel itself failed — not a path issue at this point, it found the file but the hash did not match.
What FIPS Noboot Actually Means

The sequence you described:

fips-boot start
[LUKS password]
fips boot done
fips noboot start
checking integrity of kernel
integrity test failed

fips boot done means dracut found and loaded the FIPS module successfully. fips noboot means the kernel or initramfs hash verification failed against the stored .hmac file.
The Real Problem

FIPS mode requires a .hmac file alongside every kernel and initramfs:

/boot/vmlinuz-6.x.x-xxx
/boot/.vmlinuz-6.x.x-xxx.hmac   ← must exist
/boot/initramfs-6.x.x-xxx.img
/boot/.initramfs-6.x.x-xxx.img.hmac  ← must exist

The // and dot in the error path you saw earlier was actually FIPS trying to read the .hmac file — that leading dot is intentional, it is the hidden hmac file naming convention.
Check If hmac Files Exist

In your safe mode bash:
bash

ls -la /boot/.vmlinuz*
ls -la /boot/.initramfs*

If those files are missing or zero bytes, FIPS will always fail regardless of boot parameters.
Fix — Regenerate hmac Files
bash

# Regenerate kernel hmac
dracut --regenerate-all --force

# Or specifically for your kernel version
dracut -f /boot/initramfs-6.x.x-xxx.img 6.x.x-xxx

After dracut completes:
bash

ls -la /boot/.vmlinuz*
ls -la /boot/.initramfs*

Both should now exist with non-zero size. Then retry the boot.

