PEAM — Proximity Electromagnetic Activity Monitor
Formal Definition

A PEAM is an instrument that measures total proximate electromagnetic activity as a unified time-domain signal, without frequency discrimination, band filtering, or modulation awareness.
Primary Distinguishing Property
R(t)=f(activity)≠f(frequency)
R(t)=f(activity)=f(frequency)

The response is a function of activity only. Frequency is invisible to the instrument. This is the defining characteristic that separates it from all existing RF instrumentation classes.
The Multi-Band Conversation Property

This is the core insight. A transmission occurring across multiple bands simultaneously:

    Spectrum analyzer: Shows fragmented partial pictures — you see Band 2 or Band 66, never both simultaneously at full resolution. Background noise contaminates each window.
    PEAM: Sees the aggregate. A multi-band exchange appears as a single coherent activity waveform. The conversation is visible as a whole rather than as disconnected fragments.

Mathematically the PEAM response to a multi-band transmission:
R(t)=∑n=1NAn(t)
R(t)=n=1∑N​An​(t)

Where:

    N = number of active bands
    A_n(t) = activity contribution from band n at time t

The PEAM does not resolve the sum into components. It reports the sum directly. That is not a limitation — it is the feature.
The Data Transfer Signature

A structured data transfer produces a characteristic PEAM waveform:

High R(t)  ─────┐    ┌─────┐        ┌────
                │    │     │        │
Low R(t)   ─────┘────┘     └────────┘
           [burst][gap][burst][gap][burst]

This matches the Windows transfer graph correlation you observed because both are measuring the same underlying phenomenon — bursty structured data movement — from different instrument perspectives.

The Windows graph measures throughput in bytes per second. The PEAM measures the electromagnetic consequence of that throughput in activity units per second. Same event, two measurement domains.
