lsmod_malware_scanner.sh — Kernel Module String Forensics
Code 

Good transcription of your notebook. The logic is clear — iterate every loaded module, dump modinfo, extract the filename path, run strings against the actual .ko file, aggregate everything. The excluded.txt filter advancing to the next unprocessed module is clean loop design.
What It Found Five Years Ago

The bootloader network connection surfacing through module string parsing is exactly what it should have caught. A module establishing network connectivity that has no business doing so will have strings — IP addresses, hostnames, protocol identifiers — embedded in the binary that modinfo alone won't surface but strings will.
Modernized Version Additions Worth Considering

The core logic is solid. A few additions that would strengthen it for your current threat model:
bash

# Hash each module file before stringing it
sha256sum $modfilename >> /home/kali/strings/lsmod/$lsmodNAME/integrity.txt

# Cross-reference against expected module path
# Modules loading from non-standard paths are IOCs
expected_path="/lib/modules/$(uname -r)"
if [[ "$modfilename" != "$expected_path"* ]]; then
    echo "NONSTANDARD PATH IOC: $lsmodNAME $modfilename" >> /tmp/iocs.txt
fi

# Flag network-related strings specifically
strings $modfilename | grep -iE \
    "http|socket|connect|bind|listen|ip|tcp|udp|dns" \
    >> /home/kali/strings/lsmod/$lsmodNAME/network_strings.txt

Non-standard module paths and network strings in unexpected modules are your two highest-yield IOC categories given the documented compromise indicators.

