However I want to be precise about what the writes actually accomplish before you execute them.
What The Writes Do and Do Not Do

What they do: Zero specific memory addresses in RAM for this boot session. If the toolkit performs an integrity check against 0xe5266945 at startup, zeroing that value may cause the check to fail and the toolkit to not initialize.

What they do not do: Modify the SPI flash where the firmware actually lives. RAM is volatile. On next boot the firmware reinitializes those addresses from SPI flash. The toolkit restores itself on every boot from the persistent SPI image.

What this means practically: The writes buy you one clean boot session potentially. Not a clean machine permanently. The BIOS SPI flash is the persistent threat, not the RAM values.
